---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Grid, Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Introduction - Replenish Guide"
    next={{ name: "History", href: "/history" }}
>
    <PageHeader 
        title="Replenish Guide"
        description="<strong>DON'T PANIC.</strong><br />Welcome, weary traveler. What you are looking at is the mostly harmless, yet wholly definitive guide to playing the Replenish deck in the Premodern format. This compendium is an expanded, interactive adaptation of the <a href='https://squeeshouseofpizza.com/en-brl/pages/replenish-primer-brian-siu' target='_blank' style='color: var(--w-color-primary-20); text-decoration: underline;'>excellent primer</a> originally penned by <strong>Brian Siu (Dr Siuss)</strong>. It has been redesigned and upgraded to serve as your ultimate resource—so grab your towel, ensure your tray table is securely stowed, and let's cheat some enchantments into play."
        imageSrc="/images/banners/replenish.webp"
        imageAlt="Replenish"
        imageCaption="Replenish by Jim Nelson"
        isCard={false}
    />

    <Card title="What is Replenish?" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            <strong>Replenish</strong> is a combo-control archetype in Premodern Magic:
            The Gathering that leverages its namesake card to cheat powerful, high-cost
            enchantments into play directly from the graveyard. The primary game plan
            is simple in concept: fill your graveyard using card selection engines like
            Attunement and Frantic Search, and then cast Replenish to return them all
            to the battlefield at once for just four mana.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1.25rem;">
            The Core Combo: The Wave Lock
        </h3>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
            The deck's primary win condition revolves around a devastating synergy
            between two cards:
        </p>
        <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Opalescence:</strong> Turns all of your non-Aura enchantments
                into creatures with power and toughness equal to their mana value.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Parallax Wave:</strong> Normally used to temporarily exile up
                to five creatures. However, because Opalescence turns Wave into a creature,
                <em>it can target itself</em>.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            By stacking exile triggers targeting your opponent's board and then
            using the final counter to exile the Parallax Wave itself, the Wave
            leaves play <em>before</em> its earlier triggers resolve. Due to old rules
            text, this severs the return trigger—meaning your opponent's creatures are
            exiled permanently, while your Wave returns to the battlefield with 5 fresh
            counters. Add a <strong>Decree of Silence</strong> to this loop, and you can
            counter every spell your opponent tries to cast. That's the lock.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Why Replenish?" titleTag="h2">
    <p
        style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); line-height: 1.6;"
    >
        If you are looking for a deck capable of explosive, unfair Turn-4 combo
        kills, but that can simultaneously grind through long, grueling control
        matchups with heavy interaction and card advantage, Replenish is the
        deck for you.
    </p>
    <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            You enjoy combo decks that require careful setup, patience, and
            reward you with an explosive payoff.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            You appreciate intricate sequencing, deep decision trees, and the
            puzzle of navigating to victory from complex board states.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            You want a highly adaptable deck that seamlessly pivots between
            combo, control, and midrange roles depending on the matchup.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            You want to punish metagames heavily skewed towards Enchantress,
            Midrange, or Aggro.
        </li>
        </ul>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Strengths and Weaknesses" titleTag="h2">
        <Grid column={{ md: 2 }} gap="md" style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <Card>
            <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-success);">
                Strengths
            </h3>
            <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Explosive Wins:</strong> A single resolved Replenish assembling
                    the Opal-Wave-Decree lock usually ends the game on the spot.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Role Flexibility:</strong> It can play as combo, control,
                    or midrange depending on the matchup. Tools like Meddling Mage
                    provide excellent proactive disruption.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Dominant Matchups:</strong> Highly favored against the
                    format's pillars, including Enchantress, midrange piles, creature
                    strategies, and most control variants.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Post-Ban Positioning:</strong> With Parallax Tide banned,
                    opponents no longer have access to it either, freeing up significant
                    deckbuilding space for your own answers.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </Card>

        <Card>
            <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-danger);">
                Weaknesses
            </h3>
            <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Graveyard Dependence:</strong> Inherently vulnerable to
                    hate pieces like Tormod's Crypt, Phyrexian Furnace, and Planar
                    Void (though post-sideboard creature plans mitigate this).
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Multi-Axis Aggression:</strong> Struggles against decks
                    that apply pressure on two fronts simultaneously (e.g., a fast
                    clock paired with mana denial or heavy countermagic).
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Faster Unfair Combos:</strong> Structurally weak to archetypes
                    like Devourer Combo that can goldfish a win faster than you can
                    meaningfully interact.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Mana Hungry:</strong> Key pieces cost three to four mana,
                    meaning you must survive the critical early turns before your
                    card selection engines come online.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </Card>
    </Grid>
    </Card>
</Layout>
